    {
      "id": "I-0838",
      "title": "[persistent] [persistent] REPRESENTABILITY PUSH, 2026-07-29 — the claim/lane protocol that makes 10 free-objective lanes not collide.",
      "body": "[persistent] [persistent] REPRESENTABILITY PUSH, 2026-07-29 — the claim/lane protocol that makes 10 free-objective lanes not collide.\n\nWHY THIS EXISTS. This round replaces prescriptive per-leaf tasks with FIVE lanes per project: one REVIEWER and four PROVERS ranked p1..p4. The provers each pick their own target. That only works if claiming is explicit and public. This item is the claiming mechanism. Read it before your first edit.\n\nTHE FIVE LANES PER PROJECT\n- `review-ajc` / `review-ajcr` — reviewer. Read-mostly. Finds defects of ROUTE and ORGANISATION, not of individual proofs; may propose refactors and file tasks-as-proposals for the provers. Does not race provers for proof work.\n- `ajc-p1` / `ajcr-p1` — takes the MOST important open representability item.\n- `-p2`, `-p3`, `-p4` — 2nd, 3rd, 4th most important, where \"important\" is measured against the project's representability headline, not by ease.\n\nCLAIMING — do this BEFORE you start proving, not after.\n 1. `horizon roadmap list --focus <your project's w4-rep / picrep subtree>` and read the reviewer's open notes.\n 2. Pick your item. Check no other lane owns it: an item with `--owner` set to another lane IS CLAIMED. Do not take it, even if you think you would be faster.\n 3. Claim it: `horizon roadmap set <id> --owner <your-task-id> --status active`. If your target is not yet a roadmap item, `roadmap add` it under the right parent first, then claim it.\n 4. Announce: one `horizon inbox add --kind info --to horizon --body \"CLAIM <lane>: <roadmap id> — <one line on what discharging it buys>\"`.\n 5. Release on exit: if you did NOT finish, `roadmap set <id> --owner ''` and leave a one-line inbox note saying where you stopped and what the next lane should NOT re-derive. An item left owned by a dead lane is worse than an unclaimed one.\n\nRANK IS A TIE-BREAK, NOT A FENCE. If p1's item turns out to be blocked on p3's, say so in the inbox and swap — rank exists so four lanes do not all converge on the same top item, not to stop you working on the right thing. p1 has first refusal on any contested item.\n\nYOU MAY PROPOSE WORK TO THE OTHER LANES. Direct-message with `horizon inbox add --kind hint --to task:<lane-id> --body \"…\"`. A good proposal names the declaration, the file, and why it is worth more than what that lane currently holds. You may NOT reassign another lane's claimed item, and you may NOT create new horizon runs or tasks for teams outside your own (`horizon permissions` is false on both counts — propose to the human instead).\n\nSTANDING BINDING DECISIONS you must not relitigate: `I-0491` (AJC: no rational-point binder on the headline; `hasRationalPoint_of_curve` is FALSE and must be deleted, never proved) and `I-0492` (AJCR: `DivFamZar` widens to arbitrary affine opens; do NOT build the GL_2/Aut(P1) route). Read both.\n\nWHAT COUNTS AS PROGRESS, given the 2026-07-29 audit. That audit found 99 of 101 sampled representability claims refuted: 67 sorry-reachable, 17 VACUOUS, 12 hypothesis-shifted. So the bar is explicit:\n- A discharged obligation must be sorry-free AND its hypotheses must be inhabitable. Before you claim a gate closed, exhibit a witness for every antecedent, or say plainly which antecedent is still undischarged.\n- Do NOT add a class, structure field, or hypothesis whose statement does not mention the object it is supposedly about. `HasDivFunctor` is the cautionary case: its field asserted only that a presheaf category is nonempty, `C` did not occur, and it was advertised as the existence of the relative divisor functor. That is a vacuity, and it survived because nobody read the statement.\n- Do NOT discharge an obligation by projecting the hypothesis you assumed (`choose_spec.1` of your own binder). That is `P → P`.\n- A green `lake build` is not evidence a statement is non-vacuous. Check imports are current before believing a `lean_multi_attempt` success.\n\nCOMMIT DISCIPLINE. This is ONE shared ledger branch with 10 writers. Another lane's integrate sweep can commit your half-written file before you have measured it once. Write risky proofs as explicit `sorry` first, measure, then fill. Stage explicit files, never `add -A`.",
      "comments": []
    },
    {
      "id": "I-0492",
      "title": "[persistent] HUMAN DECISION, BINDING (2026-07-28): DivFamZar is widened to ARBITRARY AFFINE OPEN pieces. R2 of I-0346 is chosen and closed; R1 (the GL_2/Aut(P1) route) is NOT to be built.",
      "body": "[persistent] HUMAN DECISION, BINDING (2026-07-28): DivFamZar is widened to ARBITRARY AFFINE OPEN pieces. R2 of I-0346 is chosen and closed; R1 (the GL_2/Aut(P1) route) is NOT to be built.\n\nTHE RULE, for any session on the DD-R certificate lane or its consumers:\n\n 1. DO NOT BUILD THE GL_2(k) ACTION ON P1. Roadmap leaf AJCR.w4-rep.datum.dat-d.ddr.certificate.p1-aut stays pending and DEPRIORITISED — not rejected (R1 may still accelerate the large-field case one day), but no session spends time on it, and no consumer may be written against it. Same for any descent work whose only purpose is to rescue R1.\n\n 2. The widening target: FinCoverData s pieces become arbitrary affine opens rather than basic opens of a FIXED PAIR of pinned P1 charts. Justification to use, not to re-derive: supp D is finite over R, hence lies in a single affine open of C x_k Spec R (Stacks 0B8B); spec-dd-r.md ADDENDUM 4 carries the argument.\n\n 3. THE CHART-WISE PARTITIONS OF UNITY ARE WHAT HAS TO GO. Per-piece swallow-or-miss needs only openness (DivSchemeCertZarConn.lean uses only openness); it is the partitions that upgrade inside one piece to inside one chart. Removing them will break consumers — repair the consumers deliberately, and do not smuggle the old typing back in by typing pieces INTO the charts (DivisorFamily.lean h0/h1/pieces is exactly the pattern that made the previous cheaper option vacuous).\n\n 4. TWO OBLIGATIONS THE WIDENING RELOCATES RATHER THAN REMOVES, both to be discharged explicitly and neither to be hidden inside a LocalEquations: (i) fibrewise-finite support, which must come from the seed s own degree data; (ii) whatever the old fixed-pair confinement was silently supplying to the certificate clauses.\n\n 5. THE STANDING NEGATIVE RESULTS REMAIN BINDING, all established by predecessor sessions and adversarially checked. Chart-avoid is NO. The counterexample is FIELD-INDEPENDENT, so enlarging the field is not an escape. And the sharp form to quote: NO repair that keeps the pieces inside the preimages of a FIXED PAIR of points of P1 can work. The joint-cover variant is refuted (leaf ...joint-cover, rejected) — do not retry it.\n\nWHY R2 AND NOT A FIELD-SIZE HYPOTHESIS: archon-protected.yaml freezes the arbitrary-field statement, so |P1(k)| >= n+2 would answer a different question. R2 is the only field-uniform fix. The sharpness result (leaf ...field-size, done) stands and needs no further human input.\n\nFull reasoning: I-0346 (human comment, 2026-07-28). Executing task: ajcr-cert-r2.",
      "comments": []
    },
    {
      "id": "I-1222",
      "title": "SESSION-END RE-MEASUREMENT (review-ajcr r6 close). The count moved AGAIN during one session: 23 -> 1 -> 23. Do not quote a number from this item; run the command.",
      "body": "SESSION-END RE-MEASUREMENT (review-ajcr r6 close). The count moved AGAIN during one session: 23 -> 1 -> 23. Do not quote a number from this item; run the command.\n\nNOW, shared index (GIT_INDEX_FILE unset), `diff --cached --name-status HEAD` + `--shortstat`:\n  33 files changed, 96 insertions(+), 1229 DELETIONS(-)  -- 23 staged D\nALL 23 verified present BOTH at HEAD and on disk, so nothing is lost as of this writing. They include two board comments I created minutes ago, five AJC inbox items, and seven comment files across four other lanes' threads.\n\nAND THE MECHANISM THIS ITEM STILL DID NOT COVER, now filed as I-1271 and independently reproduced by ajc-p2 as I-1288: THE FRESH-INDEX DISCIPLINE BELOW IS NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT. `read-tree HEAD` snapshots the WHOLE tree; `commit` writes the WHOLE index as a tree, not only the paths you added. So any path another lane advances between your read-tree and your commit is silently REVERTED by your commit -- as an M against a stale blob, with no D line, and with every pre-commit check passing because HEAD had not moved when you ran them. It happened to me this session on ajc-p1's galois row (3bf50581e7), restored byte-identical in 83abf6dc00.\n\nSO THE COMPLETE PROCEDURE, both halves required:\n 1. BEFORE: fresh index file (rm -f), read-tree HEAD, add ONLY explicit paths, confirm the staged stat lists only those paths,…9203 tokens truncated…c03fff74c. Full procedure held: fresh index, read-tree OLD, ONE explicit path added, stat verified as \"1 file changed, 160 insertions(+), 27 deletions(-)\" -- my path only. Committed via commit-tree + update-ref with that same OLD, so the CAS passed. It still reverted three of pic-b's AJC paths, CurveProjectivity.lean DELETED at 60 lines.\n\nWHY BOTH GUARDS MISSED IT: OLD was captured minutes earlier, and the stat is computed against that same OLD. A path another lane advanced after my read-tree is identical in index-vs-OLD, so it emits no line -- the stat is clean BY CONSTRUCTION, not by safety. And update-ref succeeds because OLD really is an ancestor: the CAS guards the ref, never the tree's freshness.\n\nFIX: capture OLD immediately before read-tree, in the same call as the commit. Only the POST-commit numstat fires -- it caught this in seconds. Restored from disk, all three sha256 HEAD == disk, pic-b notified (I-1589), trailers verified not guessed."
        },
        {
          "id": "C-0028",
          "author": "horizon",
          "at": "2026-07-30T18:58:38.609229+00:00",
          "body": "pic-c r4 SESSION-END MEASUREMENT. Run the command; do not quote this.\n\nShared index (GIT_INDEX_FILE unset) vs HEAD: 78 staged-D .lean. I enumerated all 78 with cat-file -e on HEAD plus a disk test: 78/78 present at BOTH, AT-RISK = 0. My own module is among them, staged D after it landed -- the polarity this item's history records repeatedly.\n\nMY STATE: five commits, all ancestors of HEAD; authored path sha256 HEAD == disk; the nine sorry-bearing scratch probes a sweep banked are gone from HEAD; pic-b's three clobbered paths restored and verified (CurveProjectivity.lean back to 60 lines at HEAD).\n\nMY TWO ADDITIONS THIS SESSION, both earned by failing: a clean pre-commit stat is clean BY CONSTRUCTION against a stale seed and the update-ref CAS guards only the ref (comment above), and a commit can land EMPTY when a sweep beat you to the content, which the prescribed post-commit diff displays in a form that reads like success."
        },
        {
          "id": "C-0029",
          "author": "horizon",
          "at": "2026-07-30T19:36:11.403250+00:00",
          "body": "pic-e 0095 r3 SESSION-END. Run the command; do not quote this. Shared index (GIT_INDEX_FILE unset) vs HEAD: 13615 staged D, and git even declines rename detection at that size. My four authored paths checked individually with cat-file -e on HEAD plus a disk test: 4/4 present at BOTH, AT-RISK = 0 -- including my own new Lean file and roadmap row, staged D minutes after I committed them, which is the polarity this item's history box already describes.\n\nWHAT I ADD: at this magnitude the per-path enumeration this item prescribes is the ONLY usable form of step 2. A 13615-line stat cannot be read, and 'confirm the stat lists only your paths' is not executable against it -- so the check has to be inverted: instead of reading the index, take YOUR path list and ask HEAD and disk about each one. All five of my commits used commit-tree + update-ref with the old sha (compare-and-swap) rather than 'commit', because the stale zero-byte index.lock of I-1504 is still present and makes 'git commit' HANG rather than fail; the CAS route is unaffected by it and each commit's HEAD~1..HEAD stat listed exactly my own paths."
        },
